5. Canonical object hierarchy

We should explicitly distinguish these concepts.

Event
  │
  └── Observation
          │
          ├── Entity
          │
          └── Fact
                  │
                  └── Relationship / Graph Edge

Then:

Entity
 ├── Facts
 ├── Relationships
 ├── Observations
 ├── Episodes
 └── References

And separately:

Goal
 └── Plan
      ├── Milestone
      └── Task
           └── Execution

And:

Skill
 └── Procedure
      ├── Step
      ├── Verification
      └── Failure Mode

This prevents concepts from collapsing into one giant "memory" abstraction.

6. Canonical identity model

Every persistent MOS object gets:

object_id
object_type
created_at
updated_at
version
status
owner
provenance

UUID remains the canonical identifier.

But there is an important refinement:

Object identity ≠ version identity

For example:

fact_id = F123


version 1
version 2
version 3

The fact remains the same conceptual object.

Its versions represent its history.

Therefore:

fact_id
version_id

should be conceptually distinct.

This is important for synchronization and federation.

7. Canonical provenance model

Every knowledge-bearing object must be explainable.

The minimum provenance chain becomes:

Object
   │
   ▼
Source
   │
   ▼
Event
   │
   ▼
Actor
   │
   ▼
Timestamp

For LLM-derived information:

Fact
 │
 ├── source_event
 ├── extraction_worker
 ├── model
 ├── model_version
 ├── prompt/template version
 ├── confidence
 └── evidence

This gives us something extremely important:

MOS can distinguish what was observed from what an AI inferred from what was observed.

That distinction should be preserved permanently.

8. Confidence needs refinement

We previously used a simple:

confidence = 0.0 → 1.0

That is useful but insufficient by itself.

We should distinguish:

confidence
confidence_source
confidence_method

For example:

confidence: 0.98


basis:
explicit_user_statement

versus:

confidence: 0.72


basis:
llm_inference

versus:

confidence: 0.91


basis:
multi_source_agreement

This prevents a numerical score from pretending to have more meaning than it actually does.

9. Canonical memory states

We should freeze the lifecycle states.

active
superseded
expired
archived
quarantined
deleted

However, there is an important correction to our earlier "nothing is ever deleted" rule.

Operationally, MOS may need to delete data for:

privacy requirements,
legal requirements,
security incidents,
explicit user deletion.

Therefore the actual invariant should be:

MOS never silently destroys knowledge state. Destructive operations must be explicit, authorized, auditable where legally permissible, and distinguish logical retirement from physical deletion.

That is much more production-ready.

10. Events remain immutable

This should be an absolute invariant.

Event
  ↓
append-only

Never:

UPDATE event

Never:

rewrite event

Corrections are new events.

Example:

Event 100:
User owns laptop A


Event 150:
User sold laptop A


Event 151:
User owns laptop B

The original historical events remain intact.

11. Facts are temporal

Facts need temporal semantics.

At minimum:

valid_from
valid_until
recorded_at

These mean different things.

valid_from

When the fact became true.

valid_until

When it stopped being true.

recorded_at

When MOS learned about it.

This distinction is extremely important.

Example:

User moved to Stockholm:


valid_from = 2025-01-01
recorded_at = 2026-02-10

MOS learned it a year after it happened.

12. Graph alignment

We need to explicitly define:

Facts are semantic knowledge. Graph edges are a projection of relationships derived from facts.

Therefore:

Fact
 │
 ▼
Graph Projection
 │
 ▼
Edge

The graph must retain a reference to the fact(s) supporting it.

Therefore graph traversal can eventually answer:

Why does this edge exist?

This is one of the most important explainability guarantees in the entire system.

13. Embedding alignment

Embeddings are explicitly derived.

Entity
Fact
Episode
Document
Skill
Procedure
Summary
      │
      ▼
Embedding Worker
      │
      ▼
Embedding

Embeddings must contain:

source_object_id
model
model_version
dimension
created_at

Changing the embedding model therefore does not corrupt memory.

We simply generate:

embedding v1
embedding v2

and eventually retire the old projection.

